+## Running a Session: the Table Feed
+
+The AI GM cannot be woken by Foundry on its own, and waking it for every chat line would be slow and costly. The table feed solves both: the Foundry module records what happens at the table, and the MCP server sends one bundled message (a "beat") only when the GM is actually needed.
+
+**Start it** (Claude Code): ask Claude to start the table feed. It runs `table-watch status` for the address (default `ws://127.0.0.1:31418`) and watches it with the Monitor tool for the rest of the session.
+
+**How the chat is read**
+
+| Sent as | Effect |
+|---|---|
+| A character, in character or emote | Wakes the AI GM |
+| Private to the GM (whisper) | Wakes the AI GM |
+| Public, as the user (out of character) | Ignored: table talk |
+| Blind to the GM | Ignored |
+| Whisper between players | Ignored |
+
+**Other events**: a GM-run creature's turn in combat and a player opening a door wake the AI GM. Rolls, item and spell cards, token moves, hit point changes, players joining or leaving, combat start and end and a player's turn are not worth a wake-up on their own, so they are listed in the next beat. Rolls asked for with `request-player-rolls` are awaited: the beat comes once each of them is in (or after 90 seconds, naming who did not roll).
+
+**Timing**: a beat goes once the table has been quiet for 5 seconds after the last waking event (1.5 seconds after a turn change), at most one every 10 seconds, and at the latest 25 seconds after the first waking event. Each beat carries a snapshot of the table (scene, combat order with hit points and conditions, the party, who is online). `table-watch settings` changes these timings.
+
+**Mute**: the module settings have **Mute the AI GM (table feed)** for breaks and long banter. Events keep being recorded and arrive in one beat when it is turned off again. **Wake the AI GM when a player opens a door** can be turned off too.
+
